# y2q file contexts.
#
# y2q runs Samsung's prebuilt NFC / secure-element HALs, whose binary names do not
# match the AOSP ones that sm8250-common labels in sepolicy/vendor/file_contexts
# (android.hardware.nfc-service.sec-sku and .secure_element-service.nxp /
# .thales-sku).  Without an entry here both binaries inherit the generic
# u:object_r:vendor_file:s0 and init REFUSES to start their services outright:
#
#   init: Could not ctl.start for 'vendor.nfc_hal_service' from pid: ... :
#   File /vendor/bin/hw/nxp.android.hardware.nfc@1.2-service
#   (labeled "u:object_r:vendor_file:s0") has incorrect label or no domain
#   transition from u:r:init:s0 to another SELinux domain defined.
#
# The consequence is that the HAL is never registered with hwservicemanager, so
# com.android.nfc finds no android.hardware.nfc@1.2::INfc/default, dies, is
# restarted by the ActivityManager, and NFC stays dead - verified with
#   ls -Z /vendor/bin/hw/   ->  u:object_r:vendor_file:s0
# on both binaries while every other HAL was labelled correctly.
#
# The domains themselves already exist in sm8250-common; only the labelling was
# missing.  Keep the regexes anchored like the common file does.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why the launchers are listed too
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running the HALs directly turned out not to be enough: their blobs need
# libnfc_trim_shim.so preloaded, but init's `user nfc` / `user secure_element`
# makes the kernel set AT_SECURE, and bionic's linker then ignores LD_PRELOAD
# (linker_main.cpp:430-432).  The HALs therefore died with
#
#   CANNOT LINK EXECUTABLE ... cannot locate symbol
#     "_ZN7android4base4TrimERKNSt3__1..." referenced by nfc_nci_nxpsn.so
#
# The services now point at the two launchers built from nfc-shim/, which drop to
# the AID themselves and only then exec the real binary with LD_PRELOAD set.
#
# The launchers get the same exec type as the binaries they replace, so init
# still transitions straight into hal_nfc_default / hal_secure_element_default and
# the exec they perform stays inside that same domain - no extra policy, and no
# domain transition at the second exec.
#
# The real binaries keep their /vendor/bin/hw names and their entries above, so
# executing them from those domains remains allowed.
/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/nxp\.android\.hardware\.nfc@1\.2-service          u:object_r:hal_nfc_default_exec:s0


/(vendor|system/vendor)/bin/hw/android\.hardware\.secure_element@1\.1-service    u:object_r:hal_secure_element_default_exec:s0

